WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:06.839
In 2015 Antonio Guillem takes another stock
photo, just like he has thousands of times

00:00:06.839 --> 00:00:07.899
before.

00:00:07.899 --> 00:00:12.061
Two years later several people use this image
to make jokes, but it’s soon lost in the

00:00:12.061 --> 00:00:13.200
sea of internet memes.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:19.340
Then, on August 19th, twitter user n1m161
makes this meme, joking about young people’s

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:21.210
affinity for socialism.

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:24.200
The post goes a little twitter viral, getting
a few thousand retweets, and suddenly, the

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:29.530
collective hive-mind of the internet has decided
that it’s found it’s new favorite meme.

00:00:29.530 --> 00:00:31.270
For now.

00:00:31.270 --> 00:00:33.880
Why do some memes capture our attention?

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:39.170
What causes a meme’s meteoric rise to the
top of internet culture at a given moment?

00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:40.620
I’ve got a few ideas.

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:43.399
Thank you to Squarespace for sponsoring this
video.

00:00:43.399 --> 00:00:49.280
Memes have been growing in notoriety in recent
years, I’ve noticed people, even those who

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:53.079
aren’t deep into internet culture talk about
“a meme they saw”.

00:00:53.079 --> 00:00:56.699
When most people use the word meme today they’re
referring to an image macro.

00:00:56.699 --> 00:00:59.149
These internet memes usually have two parts.

00:00:59.149 --> 00:01:04.250
An image that serves as a caricature or symbolic
representation, and a recognizable formatting

00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:08.120
of text, used to make a joke in conjunction
with the image.

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:12.610
But to understand a successful internet meme
we’ll need to dig a little into where the

00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:23.050
word “meme” comes from.

00:01:23.050 --> 00:01:28.770
This is Richard Dawkins, who coined the term
meme in his 1976 book “The Selfish Gene”:

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:34.470
Though he coined the term before internet
memes existed, the name fits, because Dawkins’

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:38.740
idea of meme is an almost perfect description
of how an internet meme works.

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:44.930
There’s a cultural “survival of the fittest”
process that helps create and spread successful

00:01:44.930 --> 00:01:51.240
memes.

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:59.090
The 
process selects for the appeal of the meme,

00:01:59.090 --> 00:02:03.350
and the ability of the image and joke format
to be re-applied to many topics.

00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:08.200
Memes are chosen by a sub culture, or by a
larger group, because they resonate.

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:10.299
But what causes the resonance?

00:02:10.299 --> 00:02:14.900
Let’s use the recently popular “Distracted
Boyfriend” meme as our guiding example.

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:19.650
A successful meme expresses a certain idea
or concept more quickly than that idea could

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:20.909
be articulated.

00:02:20.909 --> 00:02:24.879
If you look at the page for this stock photo
on Shutterstock you’ll see the full title

00:02:24.879 --> 00:02:29.920
of “Disloyal man walking with his girlfriend
and looking amazed at another seductive girl.”

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:35.720
It takes a lot of words to describe this scenario,
but when you look at this image you know immediately

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:36.880
what’s going on.

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:40.540
The characters have a past, there’s a future
fallout from this moment.

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:45.290
But more importantly there is a core feeling
that is time consuming to lay out in words,

00:02:45.290 --> 00:02:48.240
but that is immediately evident when viewing
the photo.

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:51.480
In this way it behaves as an archetypal image.

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:57.329
An Archetype is a deeply felt, instinctual
pre-idea form embedded in the collective unconscious.

00:02:57.329 --> 00:03:02.700
And while the archetype itself cannot be defined
or expressed explicitly, we can recognize

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:06.049
it when it’s represented within an archetypal
image.

00:03:06.049 --> 00:03:11.250
The archetype embedded in this image is a
complex scenario, recognized by all of us

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:15.170
easily, it can be described by the phrase
“the grass is always greener on the other

00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:16.439
side of the fence.”

00:03:16.439 --> 00:03:21.840
But even using that phrase is resorting to
use of symbolism to represent this scenario.

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:27.489
A good meme succeeds because it taps into
the past experiences of the collective and

00:03:27.489 --> 00:03:33.689
as it spreads becomes a part of the collective
symbolism for those experiences.

00:03:33.689 --> 00:03:39.999
That’s Stefan Hayden, a Shutterstock developer
I spoke with who’s working on an image editing

00:03:39.999 --> 00:04:01.239
tool that lets people quickly create their
own versions of the meme.

00:04:01.239 --> 00:04:04.680
This is why stock images tend to make great
memes.

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:09.620
Images that end up being used in successful
memes often have characters whose expressions

00:04:09.620 --> 00:04:11.019
are exaggerated.

00:04:11.019 --> 00:04:15.590
They appear as caricatures for the emotion
or feeling they are expressing.

00:04:15.590 --> 00:04:21.370
Those exaggerated elements make the meme stronger,
it’s symbolism is more easily detected by

00:04:21.370 --> 00:04:22.650
more people.

00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:28.170
And the exaggerated effect is funny, we like
it for the same reason we like cartoons.

00:04:28.170 --> 00:04:33.699
This isn’t to suggest that a meme has to
be gotten by everyone.

00:04:33.699 --> 00:04:40.790
A meme can appeal to small group.

00:04:40.790 --> 00:04:45.650
Sometimes they have appeal because only a
small group recognized the symbolism of a

00:04:45.650 --> 00:04:46.650
meme.

00:04:46.650 --> 00:04:50.930
Memes often act as a sort of “in group”
identifier, like inside jokes, and tv show

00:04:50.930 --> 00:04:55.310
references, a meme is a way of discovering
who we have a common base of understanding

00:04:55.310 --> 00:04:56.310
with.

00:04:56.310 --> 00:05:02.129
And their short but intense lifespans make
them signposts for eras of internet culture.

00:05:02.129 --> 00:05:06.349
The perfect meme often seems to be one where
the image element is broad enough that many

00:05:06.349 --> 00:05:11.200
people “get” the symbolism, while the
joke conveyed through text has a very narrow

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:12.200
appeal.

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:15.270
Let’s examine the modes in which a meme
can interact with culture.

00:05:15.270 --> 00:05:16.270
Initial exposure.

00:05:16.270 --> 00:05:19.430
The first mode is how the meme interacts with
the viewer upon the first exposure.

00:05:19.430 --> 00:05:22.940
With the “Distracted Boyfriend” meme,
the first time you see it you find it funny

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:27.639
because of the way someone modified it to
make a joke, but also because the photo itself

00:05:27.639 --> 00:05:28.639
is funny.

00:05:28.639 --> 00:05:29.639
Some memes are never replicate, and operate
only in this mode.

00:05:29.639 --> 00:05:30.639
Continued application.

00:05:30.639 --> 00:05:32.379
In the second mode a meme’s ability to be
remixed is crucial.

00:05:32.379 --> 00:05:34.830
This is the mode where the meme gains the
most traction.

00:05:34.830 --> 00:05:40.050
A good meme has a formula that makes the application
of the core archetype to a many different

00:05:40.050 --> 00:05:41.720
scenarios easy.

00:05:41.720 --> 00:05:45.889
In this mode most of the humor is generated
from seeing all the ways in which people can

00:05:45.889 --> 00:05:46.889
use the meme.

00:05:46.889 --> 00:05:47.889
Subversion of the formula.

00:05:47.889 --> 00:05:52.080
In the third mode, humor and freshness are
generated by the subversion of the established

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:53.620
formula of the meme.

00:05:53.620 --> 00:05:57.819
Now that most of the possible applications
are exhausted, people begin to modify the

00:05:57.819 --> 00:06:03.080
original nature of the meme and use it to
express ideas that are similar, or even the

00:06:03.080 --> 00:06:04.720
inverse of the original archetype.

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:05.720
Acceptance into cultural lexicon.

00:06:05.720 --> 00:06:08.170
Ultimately a meme can enter the cultural lexicon.

00:06:08.170 --> 00:06:13.659
It can become a tool or a short of shorthand
that we might use to refer to the archetype

00:06:13.659 --> 00:06:15.349
at the core of the meme.

00:06:15.349 --> 00:06:18.050
Most memes won’t operate within all these
modes.

00:06:18.050 --> 00:06:22.720
But successful memes that stick around usually
operate well in multiple modes.

00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:25.180
Types of memes existed before the internet.

00:06:25.180 --> 00:06:28.599
But the internet has had a big influence on
memes

00:06:28.599 --> 00:06:34.159
First, by increasing the number of connections
and points of communication, it speeds up

00:06:34.159 --> 00:06:35.900
the evolution of memes.

00:06:35.900 --> 00:06:40.740
Second, the internet it makes this evolutionary
process much more visible than it’s been

00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:41.740
in the past.

00:06:41.740 --> 00:06:48.080
Before the internet tracking little know non-image
memes out in culture was extremely difficult.

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:52.840
Although for the most part, memes remain enigmatic,
and difficult to track.

00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:54.159
But maybe it’s worth the effort.

00:06:54.159 --> 00:06:58.800
I think we underestimate the importance of
what meme represent.

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:04.300
The individual meme might seem trivial, but
tracking trends across many memes reveal the

00:07:04.300 --> 00:07:08.439
deeply felt ideas in internet culture that
they represent.

00:07:08.439 --> 00:07:13.240
We can learn something about ourselves by
tracking which memes survive.

00:07:13.240 --> 00:07:18.080
At the very least, become an evolutionary
force that helps shape our culture, and share

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:19.080
a meme.

